    },
    "body": "Labs were stopping with pushed branches, unresolved reviews and incompatible consumer interfaces. Five PRs also merged during reconciliation, including policy #1522 before its coordinator/council receipts existed. This change gives every lab a complete continuing goal and one integration queue, preserves the branch inventory, and corrects the policy state so protected delivery can resume.\n\n- Existing work receives an owner/reviewer/next action and merged, successor or retained-dormant disposition. Default work in progress is one implementation and one review; goals continue through delivery and the next useful task.\n- D17 records the owner’s direct clarification: conversion must remove the transformer architecture. Passing integer-kernel checks alone is insufficient.\n- The prospective parity rule has disjoint cases and an explicit degradation sign, tie behavior and third-draw decision. Retention is separate from advantage/promotion. The older council schedule remains a historical recommendation; claims are atomic and stewardship transferable.\n- D17 proposes a one-use, exact-head council repair for #1529: the already merged unreviewed policy makes the current coordinator reject delivery, while policy migration requires the correcting PR already merged. Preserve that rejection; require real documentation checks, two non-author exact-head reviews and three recorded seats; use only the normal protected GitHub queue with a pinned head, then verified AdoptPolicy. No admin merge, direct-main push, fabricated status, source/model change or execution admission is included.\n\nValidation: documentation-only diff and claim-wording scan at the final head; independent reviews and the explicit council receipt are required before queue admission. Branch/source review findings are not model execution evidence. The existing source implementation is unchanged. #1522’s missing pre-merge gate remains recorded rather than retroactively marked passed.\n\nReferences #820, #1508, #1509, #1510, #1520, #1525.\n",
